WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.276 --> 00:00:02.834
- I don't think of myself as a boss.

00:00:02.834 --> 00:00:06.434
I think of myself as someone
who's giving direction.

00:00:06.434 --> 00:00:07.753
I think it's healthy for everyone

00:00:07.753 --> 00:00:10.563
to lose a job as well as to gain a job.

00:00:10.563 --> 00:00:12.674
I like people who represent the best

00:00:12.674 --> 00:00:14.382
of what they do and if that turns you

00:00:14.382 --> 00:00:16.496
into a perfectionist, then maybe I am.

00:00:16.496 --> 00:00:18.756
I think the more you go
out, the more you see,

00:00:18.756 --> 00:00:20.200
the more you experience life,

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.908
the more you bring back to your day job.

00:00:22.908 --> 00:00:24.964
- She's a British journalist and editor.

00:00:24.964 --> 00:00:28.292
She's been Editor in
Chief of Vogue since 1988.

00:00:28.292 --> 00:00:29.628
She's Anna Wintour,

00:00:29.628 --> 00:00:32.161
and here is my take on her
Top 10 Rules For Success.

00:00:32.161 --> 00:00:33.621
Rule number five is my personal favorite,

00:00:33.621 --> 00:00:34.992
and I'm curious to figure out which one

00:00:34.992 --> 00:00:36.909
you guys like the best.

00:00:40.117 --> 00:00:41.451
- I try and be decisive.

00:00:41.451 --> 00:00:43.831
I try even if I don't know
what the hell I'm doing,

00:00:43.831 --> 00:00:46.351
I try to be decisive because I think

00:00:46.351 --> 00:00:49.969
it's very comforting and reassuring

00:00:49.969 --> 00:00:52.527
and important to the
people that work with you

00:00:52.527 --> 00:00:55.589
to feel that whoever is in charge knows

00:00:55.589 --> 00:00:57.718
what they're doing and
knows what they're thinking.

00:00:57.718 --> 00:00:58.897
That if it's a yes, it's a yes,

00:00:58.897 --> 00:01:00.011
and if it's a no, it's a no.

00:01:00.011 --> 00:01:02.738
I mean I don't think of myself as a boss.

00:01:02.738 --> 00:01:06.055
I think of myself as someone
who's giving direction,

00:01:06.055 --> 00:01:08.305
guidance, and vice-a-versa.

00:01:09.761 --> 00:01:12.950
And, I also know careerwise how important

00:01:12.950 --> 00:01:16.276
it is not to be too highly specialized.

00:01:16.276 --> 00:01:19.158
Especially these days
in the world of media.

00:01:19.158 --> 00:01:22.593
It is crucial to have multiple
interests and skill sets.

00:01:22.593 --> 00:01:25.213
To have tried and possibly failed

00:01:25.213 --> 00:01:27.490
at many different undertakings.

00:01:27.490 --> 00:01:30.211
The young people that we
hire today at Conde Nast,

00:01:30.211 --> 00:01:32.165
are fearless polymaths.

00:01:32.165 --> 00:01:36.106
Hardworking and passionate,
creative and curious.

00:01:36.106 --> 00:01:38.563
- You eventually joined
Harper's Bazaar U.S.

00:01:38.563 --> 00:01:41.480
And in 1976 it was, you were fired,

00:01:42.949 --> 00:01:44.775
and I find that extraordinary.

00:01:44.775 --> 00:01:47.851
- (chuckles) No, they were
very clear in that they thought

00:01:47.851 --> 00:01:51.059
that I would never understand
the American market.

00:01:51.059 --> 00:01:55.226
I remember, I was the editor
on one shoot that was,

00:01:57.214 --> 00:01:59.475
I think it was the French fashion shows,

00:01:59.475 --> 00:02:02.887
and I decided that it would be interesting

00:02:02.887 --> 00:02:06.001
to have dreadlocks on the models.

00:02:06.001 --> 00:02:10.168
I think that that pushed them
further than they could bear.

00:02:12.862 --> 00:02:14.225
That was it for me.

00:02:14.225 --> 00:02:17.225
But I think it's always part of life

00:02:19.560 --> 00:02:22.127
to have some knocks as
well as some successes.

00:02:22.127 --> 00:02:23.009
- And to take risks.

00:02:23.009 --> 00:02:25.427
- And you know, to learn.

00:02:25.427 --> 00:02:27.729
So I think it's healthy for everyone

00:02:27.729 --> 00:02:31.055
to lose a job as well as to gain a job.

00:02:31.055 --> 00:02:32.189
- [Interviewer] What bores you?

00:02:32.189 --> 00:02:33.296
- Mediocrity.

00:02:33.296 --> 00:02:36.367
If you see a collection that is,

00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:37.786
you feel the designer has been lazy

00:02:37.786 --> 00:02:41.203
or taking inspiration
from other designers.

00:02:41.203 --> 00:02:44.669
It doesn't so much as bore me as anger me.

00:02:44.669 --> 00:02:46.107
- What is your best piece of advice

00:02:46.107 --> 00:02:48.547
for the next generation's influencers?

00:02:48.547 --> 00:02:51.030
- I think it's really
to be true to yourself,

00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:53.780
and listen as much as one is able

00:02:54.738 --> 00:02:58.802
to other people who's opinions you respect

00:02:58.802 --> 00:03:01.302
and look up to, but in the end

00:03:03.262 --> 00:03:05.091
it has to come from you

00:03:05.091 --> 00:03:08.092
and you can't really worry too much

00:03:08.092 --> 00:03:11.304
by looking to the left
or the right about what

00:03:11.304 --> 00:03:12.681
the competition might be doing,

00:03:12.681 --> 00:03:14.908
or other people in your
field might be doing.

00:03:14.908 --> 00:03:17.499
It has to be a true vision.

00:03:17.499 --> 00:03:20.826
- The blurb about your
unauthorized biography reads,

00:03:20.826 --> 00:03:24.331
"She's ambitious, driven,
needy, a perfectionist.

00:03:24.331 --> 00:03:26.173
"An inside look at the competitive

00:03:26.173 --> 00:03:29.090
"bitch-eat-bitch world of fashion."

00:03:30.055 --> 00:03:31.302
Accurate?

00:03:31.302 --> 00:03:33.275
- Well I'm very driven by what I do.

00:03:33.275 --> 00:03:35.206
I am certainly very competitive.

00:03:35.206 --> 00:03:37.300
What else am I needy?

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.857
I'm probably very needy, yes.

00:03:39.857 --> 00:03:40.784
A bitch?

00:03:40.784 --> 00:03:42.415
- Perfectionist.
- Perfectionist.

00:03:42.415 --> 00:03:43.444
- Oh that's right, bitch first.

00:03:43.444 --> 00:03:45.664
(laughing)

00:03:45.664 --> 00:03:47.837
- Well, I hope I'm not.

00:03:47.837 --> 00:03:49.894
I try not to be,

00:03:49.894 --> 00:03:53.713
but I like people who represent
the best of what they do.

00:03:53.713 --> 00:03:56.915
If that turns you into
perfectionist, then maybe I am.

00:03:56.915 --> 00:03:57.832
I don't ...

00:03:59.454 --> 00:04:00.371
make notes.

00:04:02.244 --> 00:04:05.209
I react very instinctively to everything.

00:04:05.209 --> 00:04:08.207
I'm a great believer in
traveling a great deal.

00:04:08.207 --> 00:04:10.879
I don't believe in meetings.

00:04:10.879 --> 00:04:14.183
More than two people in
a meeting is too many.

00:04:14.183 --> 00:04:17.556
(laughing) Really it's true.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:20.044
Do you know about stand ups?

00:04:20.044 --> 00:04:20.877
No, okay.

00:04:20.877 --> 00:04:23.002
So in Silicon Valley now
when you have a meeting

00:04:23.002 --> 00:04:25.753
everybody stands in a circle
like they're meditating.

00:04:25.753 --> 00:04:28.369
(audience laughing)

00:04:28.369 --> 00:04:30.369
Maybe I should try that.

00:04:31.911 --> 00:04:34.584
I think the more you go
out, the more you see,

00:04:34.584 --> 00:04:35.907
the more you experience life,

00:04:35.907 --> 00:04:39.159
the more you bring back to your day job.

00:04:39.159 --> 00:04:41.263
As I was trying to say in my remarks,

00:04:41.263 --> 00:04:43.198
I think the problem about big corporations

00:04:43.198 --> 00:04:46.671
is you get a lot of people my age

00:04:46.671 --> 00:04:49.412
trying to hold onto their jobs.

00:04:49.412 --> 00:04:52.969
Clinging onto their pensions
and their retirement,

00:04:52.969 --> 00:04:54.948
and they're not experiencing new things,

00:04:54.948 --> 00:04:57.910
and they don't like change and
they don't like disruption.

00:04:57.910 --> 00:04:59.823
So either they don't make a decision

00:04:59.823 --> 00:05:03.846
or they say no and as
a result they stagnate.

00:05:03.846 --> 00:05:08.726
It's terribly important to bring
people in from the outside,

00:05:08.726 --> 00:05:10.643
and not to remain too insular.

00:05:10.643 --> 00:05:14.252
And also to hire as many
young, bright people,

00:05:14.252 --> 00:05:16.556
like all of you, as you
can just to teach you

00:05:16.556 --> 00:05:19.566
how to think about things
completely differently.

00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:20.571
Well, I think all of us at Vogue

00:05:20.571 --> 00:05:23.268
are always looking for talent.

00:05:23.268 --> 00:05:25.552
And we're also looking for someone

00:05:25.552 --> 00:05:28.866
who has a head on their
shoulders and that understand

00:05:28.866 --> 00:05:31.829
that being talented is
obviously important,

00:05:31.829 --> 00:05:35.025
but that you also have to
have a sense of business.

00:05:35.025 --> 00:05:37.343
And that being creative and commercial

00:05:37.343 --> 00:05:40.598
are two words that can work together.

00:05:40.598 --> 00:05:43.492
It's very important to me that I look good

00:05:43.492 --> 00:05:45.191
when I go out publicly.

00:05:45.191 --> 00:05:47.746
I like looking at my
clothes rack in the morning

00:05:47.746 --> 00:05:50.087
and deciding what to pick out.

00:05:50.087 --> 00:05:51.252
I enjoy fashion.

00:05:51.252 --> 00:05:54.056
I mean, I wouldn't be
in this job if I didn't.

00:05:54.056 --> 00:05:57.499
I think clarity is one of the most

00:05:57.499 --> 00:05:59.941
important leadership
qualities that one can have.

00:05:59.941 --> 00:06:04.306
I think it's really
important to be very specific

00:06:04.306 --> 00:06:07.950
and very clear on what you
expect, and then to delegate.

00:06:07.950 --> 00:06:10.406
People respond much better when

00:06:10.406 --> 00:06:12.552
they're in a more independent environment

00:06:12.552 --> 00:06:14.040
and you're not completely breathing down

00:06:14.040 --> 00:06:16.266
their neck all the time.

00:06:16.266 --> 00:06:18.330
The other thing I think
is incredibly important is

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:21.059
to hire divas like Andre Leon Talley.

00:06:21.059 --> 00:06:23.376
Who will disagree with
you and contradict you

00:06:23.376 --> 00:06:26.026
and not yes you all the time.

00:06:26.026 --> 00:06:28.717
And not to be scared of
having people working

00:06:28.717 --> 00:06:31.162
with you that have different opinions.

00:06:31.162 --> 00:06:32.198
- Thank you so much for watching.

00:06:32.198 --> 00:06:35.044
I made this video because
Mauri Sylvester asked me to.

00:06:35.044 --> 00:06:36.120
So if there's a famous entrepreneur

00:06:36.120 --> 00:06:37.792
that you want me to profile next,

00:06:37.792 --> 00:06:39.060
leave it in the comments below

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:40.287
and I'll see what I can do.

00:06:40.287 --> 00:06:42.298
I'd also love to know which
of Anna's top 10 rules

00:06:42.298 --> 00:06:44.180
had the biggest impact on you and why.

00:06:44.180 --> 00:06:46.706
Leave it in the comments and
I will join in the discussion.

00:06:46.706 --> 00:06:49.367
Finally, want to give a shout
out to Jesse Willoughby.

00:06:49.367 --> 00:06:51.467
Jesse thank you so much for
buying the copy of my book.

00:06:51.467 --> 00:06:52.873
It really means a lot to me.

00:06:52.873 --> 00:06:54.903
For your chance at a shout
out in a future video,

00:06:54.903 --> 00:06:56.556
make sure to pick up a copy of the book,

00:06:56.556 --> 00:06:59.059
and email in your receipt
so we can keep track.

00:06:59.059 --> 00:07:00.435
Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:07:00.435 --> 00:07:03.118
Continue to believe or
whatever your one word is.

00:07:03.118 --> 00:07:04.951
And I'll see you soon.

